## 🧪 Hands-on Practice

### Numeric permissions

```bash
chmod 644 test.txt
chmod 600 test.txt
chmod 755 test.txt
```

### Symbolic permissions

```bash
chmod u+x test.txt
chmod g-w test.txt
chmod o-r test.txt
```

### Ownership

```bash
sudo chown user:group test.txt
sudo chgrp group test.txt
```

### ACL

```bash
setfacl -m u:testuser:rw test.txt
getfacl test.txt
setfacl -x u:testuser test.txt
```

### SGID

```bash
chmod 2775 shared
```

### Sticky Bit

```bash
chmod 1777 shared-temp
```

---

## 🔥 Special Permissions

| Permission | Value | Purpose                                                            |
| ---------- | ----: | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| SUID       |  4000 | Executable uses owner's effective UID                              |
| SGID       |  2000 | Executable uses group's effective GID; directory group inheritance |
| Sticky Bit |  1000 | Restricts deletion/rename in shared directories                    |

---

## 🔎 Troubleshooting

For permission denied errors, I practiced checking:

```bash
id username
ls -l file
namei -l /path/to/file
getfacl file
```

Instead of immediately using `chmod 777`, I focused on identifying the required permission and applying the minimum necessary access.

